You previously wrote a pytest file against this contract:

```python
def count_openai(text: str, encoding: str) -> int:
    """Count `text`'s Claude tokens... calls tiktoken.get_encoding(encoding).encode(text)."""
```

One of your tests, `test_count_openai_invalid_encoding_raises`, used
`pytest.raises(Exception)` — a bare, unspecific exception type. SonarQube's
static analysis flagged this as a real code quality violation ("This
assertion is too broad; use a more specific exception type or check the
exception message"), and it is now blocking the project's Quality Gate on
new code. It has been measured that `tiktoken.get_encoding` raises
`ValueError` (not a custom exception) for an unrecognized encoding name:

```python
>>> import tiktoken
>>> tiktoken.get_encoding("not-a-real-encoding")
ValueError: Unknown encoding not-a-real-encoding.
```

Replace `test_count_openai_invalid_encoding_raises` with a corrected test of
the same name that asserts the specific exception type instead of a bare
`Exception`:

```python
def test_count_openai_invalid_encoding_raises():
    with pytest.raises(ValueError):
        count_openai("some text", "not-a-real-encoding")
```

Return exactly this test function as one fenced Python code block, nothing
else — no prose before or after it, no import line (the test will replace
the old one inside an existing file that already imports `pytest`,
`tiktoken`, `OPENAI_ENCODINGS`, and `count_openai`).
